# The build context is the repo root for every service image, so anything not
# needed inside one is pure upload cost - node_modules alone is hundreds of
# megabytes that each Dockerfile installs again from the lockfile anyway.
node_modules
**/node_modules
dist
**/dist
dist-electron
release
build
coverage
.turbo
**/.turbo
.pnpm-store
.git
.github
.codegraph
storage-data
.vscode

# No image builds the desktop client, and its assets are the largest thing in
# the tree - several hundred megabytes of context uploaded for nothing. What
# comes back is only what the web client mounts: the UI source, the icon and
# the Tailwind theme. Not the Electron main process, not the packaged releases,
# and not the manifest - without that the workspace does not treat apps/desktop
# as a package, so no Electron binary is ever installed.
apps/desktop
!apps/desktop/src
!apps/desktop/public
!apps/desktop/tailwind.theme.mjs

# No image builds the Android client either, and it is the largest directory in
# the tree - half a gigabyte of Gradle output, a build cache and the AAR
# dependencies it unpacked. None of it is a workspace package (there is no
# package.json; it is Gradle), so nothing in a service image can reference it
# and nothing breaks by its absence.
#
# Unlike apps/desktop above, none of it comes back: the web client mounts the
# desktop's React source, and there is no equivalent share with the phone.
apps/android

# Build output anywhere, not only at the root. `build` on its own matches the
# top-level directory and nothing else, which left every nested one - a Gradle
# module's `build/`, a package's - in the context.
**/build
**/.gradle
**/out

# Claude Code keeps full worktree copies under here; they would be uploaded as
# part of the context and rebuilt into every image. `.agent` and `.agents` are
# the same kind of thing from other tools.
.claude
.agent
.agents

# Documentation, and the screenshots it links to, are not part of any image.
development
docs
pictures
*.md

# Secrets never belong in an image layer; compose injects them at run time.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
